1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,960
On the morning of 20 October 2011, a convoy
of about fifty vehicles tried to break out

2
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:19,860
of Sirte, the Libyan coastal city
where Muammar Gaddafi was born.

3
00:00:20,439 --> 00:00:24,484
He was about sixty-nine years old, and for
forty-two years he had ruled the country.

4
00:00:25,081 --> 00:00:29,586
Before the day was over he would be dead, and
exactly how he died has never been settled in public.

5
00:00:30,273 --> 00:00:35,498
This is the story of his last weeks, and of
what the official record says about his capture.

6
00:00:36,126 --> 00:00:39,806
Where accounts disagree, this
film says whose account it is.

7
00:00:40,328 --> 00:00:45,853
He took power on 1 September 1969, in
a military coup against the monarchy,

8
00:00:46,173 --> 00:00:47,133
when he was in his twenties.

9
00:00:47,861 --> 00:00:51,021
For four decades he ruled without
elections in the usual sense,

10
00:00:51,301 --> 00:00:53,401
and he called his system
a state of the masses.

11
00:00:54,188 --> 00:00:58,098
In April 1986 American aircraft
bombed Tripoli and Benghazi,

12
00:00:58,238 --> 00:01:01,698
after a bombing at a Berlin nightclub
that Washington blamed on Libya.

13
00:01:02,375 --> 00:01:07,800
On 21 December 1988, Pan Am Flight
103 exploded over Lockerbie,

14
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:10,620
in Scotland, and 270 people died.

15
00:01:11,248 --> 00:01:16,388
In 2001 a Scottish court sitting in the Netherlands
convicted a Libyan intelligence officer,

16
00:01:17,008 --> 00:01:19,248
Abdelbaset al-Megrahi, of the murders.

17
00:01:20,004 --> 00:01:24,704
In May 2002, Libya offered up to
2.7 billion dollars to the families,

18
00:01:25,024 --> 00:01:30,204
and in August 2003, it wrote to the United
Nations accepting responsibility for the actions

19
00:01:30,204 --> 00:01:31,004
of its officials.

20
00:01:31,884 --> 00:01:34,944
That December it announced it would
give up its weapons programmes,

21
00:01:35,384 --> 00:01:37,304
and western leaders
began to visit Tripoli.

22
00:01:37,975 --> 00:01:43,295
In September 2009 he addressed the United
Nations General Assembly for the first time,

23
00:01:43,515 --> 00:01:46,315
and spoke for far longer
than his allotted time.

24
00:01:46,844 --> 00:01:51,549
In early 2011, the protests that had
swept Tunisia and Egypt reached Libya.

25
00:01:57,495 --> 00:02:00,780
Protests began in Benghazi
on 15 February 2011,

26
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:04,640
and activists called a Day
of Rage for 17 February.

27
00:02:05,334 --> 00:02:10,654
The United Nations commission of inquiry later
found that security forces used live ammunition

28
00:02:10,654 --> 00:02:14,034
against protesters, and the
unrest became a civil war.

29
00:02:14,627 --> 00:02:18,467
The National Transitional Council, formed
in Benghazi at the end of February,

30
00:02:18,987 --> 00:02:21,167
became the body that
claimed to govern Libya.

31
00:02:21,824 --> 00:02:26,289
On 26 February the Security
Council passed Resolution 1970,

32
00:02:27,049 --> 00:02:30,729
which froze assets and referred Libya
to the International Criminal Court.

33
00:02:31,297 --> 00:02:36,437
On 17 March, Resolution 1973 was
adopted by ten votes to none,

34
00:02:37,117 --> 00:02:40,157
and it authorised all necessary
measures to protect civilians,

35
00:02:40,557 --> 00:02:41,837
including a no-fly zone.

36
00:02:42,517 --> 00:02:46,682
Brazil, China, Germany,
India and Russia abstained.

37
00:03:00,695 --> 00:03:03,520
NATO took command of the air
campaign at the end of March,

38
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:06,660
and the operation would
last more than seven months.

39
00:03:07,348 --> 00:03:09,808
The resolution did not mention
removing him from power,

40
00:03:10,168 --> 00:03:13,908
and Russia and China later said that
NATO had gone beyond its mandate.

41
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:19,045
On 27 June the International Criminal
Court issued arrest warrants for Gaddafi,

42
00:03:19,785 --> 00:03:24,103
his son Saif al-Islam, and his
intelligence chief Abdullah al-Senussi.

43
00:03:24,548 --> 00:03:28,973
The charges were crimes against humanity,
for the murder and persecution of civilians.

44
00:03:29,839 --> 00:03:32,334
In late August rebel
fighters entered Tripoli,

45
00:03:32,814 --> 00:03:37,394
and on 23 August they stormed
his compound at Bab al-Aziziya,

46
00:03:37,394 --> 00:03:38,494
which he had already left.

47
00:03:39,133 --> 00:03:42,353
For two months his exact
whereabouts were not publicly known.

48
00:03:42,927 --> 00:03:47,227
Loyalists held out in Sirte, and fighters
of the National Transitional Council,

49
00:03:47,587 --> 00:03:51,207
many of them from the city of
Misrata, besieged it into October.

50
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:01,320
On the morning of 20 October, a
convoy left District Two of Sirte,

51
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:04,140
the last area still held by his loyalists.

52
00:04:04,751 --> 00:04:09,351
Human Rights Watch estimates that it carried
about two hundred and fifty people in around

53
00:04:09,351 --> 00:04:10,291
fifty vehicles.

54
00:04:10,949 --> 00:04:13,909
NATO later said that it had
struck vehicles near Sirte,

55
00:04:14,209 --> 00:04:16,729
and that it had not known
Gaddafi was in the convoy.

56
00:04:17,331 --> 00:04:19,451
NATO said that it does
not target individuals,

57
00:04:20,091 --> 00:04:23,951
and that it learned only later, from
open sources and allied intelligence,

58
00:04:24,451 --> 00:04:25,331
that he had been there.

59
00:04:25,833 --> 00:04:29,493
Human Rights Watch reports that a
drone missile hit the convoy first,

60
00:04:29,833 --> 00:04:32,533
and that a warplane struck
it again near a militia base.

61
00:04:33,167 --> 00:04:36,392
The convoy broke apart, and
fighters from Misrata closed in.

62
00:04:36,933 --> 00:04:41,868
Human Rights Watch counted fifty-three
bodies and fourteen destroyed vehicles at the

63
00:04:41,868 --> 00:04:48,468
airstrike site on 21 October, and says NATO
strikes probably killed at least half of

64
00:04:48,468 --> 00:04:50,708
the more than one hundred
convoy members who died.

65
00:04:51,335 --> 00:04:55,675
Gaddafi, wounded by a grenade, was found
in a drainage culvert near the road.

66
00:04:56,255 --> 00:05:00,235
The defence minister, Abu Bakr Younis,
was killed in the same fighting.

67
00:05:00,845 --> 00:05:05,385
The United Nations commission later found
that Gaddafi was captured alive, and wounded.

68
00:05:05,948 --> 00:05:09,333
His son Mutassim was captured
separately, and was also alive.

69
00:05:09,864 --> 00:05:12,824
Fighters filmed Gaddafi on their
phones in the minutes that followed,

70
00:05:13,204 --> 00:05:16,364
and those clips were broadcast
around the world within hours.

71
00:05:22,333 --> 00:05:26,553
That day the National Transitional
Council announced that Gaddafi was dead.

72
00:05:27,114 --> 00:05:31,999
Its prime minister, Mahmoud Jibril, said he
had been hit by a bullet in the head during

73
00:05:31,999 --> 00:05:33,499
crossfire while being moved.

74
00:05:34,013 --> 00:05:37,058
Another official said that no
order had been given to kill him.

75
00:05:37,548 --> 00:05:41,708
Libya's chief pathologist said at
first that he died of a gunshot wound,

76
00:05:41,708 --> 00:05:45,668
but by October 2012 the autopsy
results had not been made public.

77
00:05:46,386 --> 00:05:50,731
In Washington, Secretary of State Hillary Clinton
was asked about the news in a CBS interview,

78
00:05:51,131 --> 00:05:53,371
and summed up the campaign
in three short clauses:

79
00:05:53,931 --> 00:05:55,911
we came, we saw, he died.

80
00:06:08,333 --> 00:06:12,713
Video taken in custody shows Gaddafi
still alive among his captors,

81
00:06:12,913 --> 00:06:14,553
before he was put into an ambulance.

82
00:06:15,308 --> 00:06:19,853
The National Transitional Council
declared Libya liberated on 23 October,

83
00:06:20,453 --> 00:06:25,153
and the next day it ordered an investigation
of his death and promised to prosecute anyone

84
00:06:25,153 --> 00:06:26,653
who killed him after capture.

85
00:06:27,325 --> 00:06:32,390
His body was taken to Misrata, and on 25
October the council said he had been buried

86
00:06:32,390 --> 00:06:34,030
in a secret grave in the desert.

87
00:06:34,603 --> 00:06:41,283
The NATO operation ended on 31 October, and
on 22 November the International Criminal

88
00:06:41,283 --> 00:06:44,383
Court closed its case against
him, because he had died.

89
00:06:44,992 --> 00:06:50,492
In March 2012 the United Nations commission
reported that Gaddafi and Mutassim were captured

90
00:06:50,492 --> 00:06:53,892
alive and later died in the
custody of fighters from Misrata.

91
00:06:54,455 --> 00:06:58,520
It said it could not determine whether
Gaddafi's death was an unlawful killing,

92
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:01,080
because it had not been
given the autopsy report,

93
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:03,080
and it called for further investigation.

94
00:07:03,848 --> 00:07:07,453
The commission could not obtain
any account of how Mutassim died,

95
00:07:07,833 --> 00:07:10,353
and so reached no conclusion
on his death either.

96
00:07:10,889 --> 00:07:15,594
In October 2012 Human Rights Watch published
a report titled Death of a Dictator,

97
00:07:15,854 --> 00:07:20,374
which concluded that he was alive in custody
and that the cause of his death was unclear.

98
00:07:21,037 --> 00:07:24,682
The same report counted at least
fifty-three bodies at a hotel in Sirte,

99
00:07:25,102 --> 00:07:27,782
and said the execution of
prisoners is a war crime.

100
00:07:28,430 --> 00:07:32,960
Human Rights Watch added that Libyan authorities
had taken no serious steps to investigate.

101
00:07:33,503 --> 00:07:38,608
In March 2012 a prosecutor in Misrata said
the investigation into Gaddafi was nearly

102
00:07:38,608 --> 00:07:44,048
finished, and in July 2012 the Libyan government
answered Human Rights Watch by attributing

103
00:07:44,048 --> 00:07:44,948
the deaths to battle.

104
00:07:45,514 --> 00:07:48,614
He had ruled for forty-two years,
and he never faced a court.

105
00:07:49,123 --> 00:07:53,423
The country he left behind has been split
between rival governments since 2014,

106
00:07:53,983 --> 00:07:57,363
and in August 2026 the
rival sides signed a deal,

107
00:07:57,683 --> 00:08:01,183
backed by the United Nations, to
hold elections within two years.

108
00:08:02,187 --> 00:08:04,187
Captured alive, dead that day.

109
00:08:04,727 --> 00:08:06,347
Who answers for what happened?
